                <h1 class="mt-4 font-display text-[30px] leading-[1.15] font-extrabold tracking-[-0.01em] text-pretty sm:text-[38px]">Add A Habit Tracker Widget To Your Home Screen</h1>

                <div class="flex flex-col gap-2 text-[13px] leading-relaxed text-muted mt-4">
    <p>By <a href="https://habitkit.app/about" rel="author" class="font-semibold text-silver hover:text-accent focus-ring">Sebastian Röhl</a>, creator of HabitKit</p>
    <p class="flex flex-wrap gap-x-3 gap-y-1">
        <span>6 min read</span>
        <span>Published <time datetime="2026-08-26">Aug 2026</time></span>
                    <span>Updated <time datetime="2026-09-04">Sep 2026</time></span>
                            <span>Instructions for HabitKit 1.18.0</span>
            </p>
</div>

                
                <div class="help-body help-lead mt-6"><p>A HabitKit widget puts one habit's grid, or a compact list of several, on your home screen, so the habit tracker is in front of you without opening the app. You add it the way you add any widget: long-press the home screen, find HabitKit in the widget picker, choose a size.</p>
<p>Home Screen Widgets are part of HabitKit Pro on both platforms, so if the one you added looks blurred or locked, that is the reason rather than a fault.</p>
<p><img src="https://habitkit.app/help-assets/charcoal-ios-widgets.webp" alt="iOS home screen panels showing HabitKit widgets, including a multi-habit grid and a smaller Coding widget" width="1600" height="900" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></p>
</div>

                <details class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-panel px-4 py-3 mt-6 lg:hidden">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer text-[14px] font-semibold text-silver focus-ring">On this page</summary>
        <nav aria-label="Article contents" class="mt-3">
            <ul class="flex list-none flex-col gap-1">
